== TO THE READER

#smallcaps[courteous companions,]

Some time ago, telling you#super[+] the dream I had of Christian the pilgrim, and of his dangerous journey towards the Celestial country, was pleasant to me and profitable to you. I also told you#super[+] then what I saw concerning his wife and children, and how unwilling they were to go with him on pilgrimage; so unwilling that he was forced to go on his progress without them, for he did not dare to run the risk of the destruction he feared would come from staying with them in the City of Destruction. So, as I showed you then, he left them and departed.

Now it has so happened, through a great press of business, that I have been much hindered and kept from my usual travels into the parts he came from, and so could not, until now, find an opportunity to inquire further after those he left behind, so that I might give you#super[+] an account of them. But lately, having had some business that way, I went down there again. Now, having taken up my lodging in a wood about a mile from the place, as I slept, I dreamed again.

And as I was in my dream, behold, an aged gentleman came by where I lay; and because he was going some part of the way I was traveling, I thought I got up and went with him. So as we walked, as travelers usually do, it seemed we fell into conversation; and our talk happened to be about Christian and his travels, for this is how I began with the old man:

Sir, I said, what town is that down there, lying on the left side of our way?

Then Mr. Sagacity (for that was his name) said, It is the City of Destruction, a populous place, but filled with a very ill-natured and idle sort of people.

I thought that was the city, I said; I once went through that town myself, and so I know that this report you give of it is true.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: Too true! I wish I could speak better of those who live there and still speak the truth.]

Well, sir, I said, then I see that you are a well-meaning man, and so one who takes pleasure in hearing and telling of what is good. Tell me, did you never hear what happened some time ago to a man of this town (whose name was Christian), who went on a pilgrimage up towards the higher regions?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: Hear of him! Yes, and I also heard of the attacks, troubles, wars, captivities, cries, groans, frights, and fears that he met with and had on his journey. Besides, I must tell you, our whole country rings with his name. There are few houses that have heard of him and his doings without having sought out and got the records of his pilgrimage; indeed, I think I may say that his hazardous journey has won many well-wishers to his ways. For though, when he was here, every man’s mouth called him a fool, now that he is gone he is highly praised by all. For it is said he lives splendidly where he is; indeed, many of those who are determined never to run his risks still have their mouths water at his gains.]

They may well think, I said, if they think anything that is true, that he lives well where he is; for he now lives at, and in, the fountain of life, and has what he has without labor and sorrow, for there is no grief mixed with it. But tell me, what do the people say about him?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: Talk! The people talk strangely about him. Some say that he now walks in white, Rev 3:4; that he has a chain of gold about his neck; that he has a crown of gold, set with pearls, on his head. Others say that the shining ones, who sometimes showed themselves to him on his journey, have become his companions, and that he is as familiar with them where he is as one neighbor is with another here. Besides, it is confidently reported of him that the King of the place where he is has already given him a very rich and pleasant dwelling at court, and that every day he eats and drinks, and walks and talks with him, and receives the smiles and favors of him who is Judge of all there. Zech 3:7; Luke 14:14, 15. Moreover, some expect that his Prince, the Lord of that country, will soon come into these parts, and will demand to know the reason, if they can give any, why his neighbors thought so little of him and mocked him so much when they saw that he would be a pilgrim. Jude 14, 15.]

For they say that he is now so much in his Prince’s affections that his Sovereign is so concerned about the insults that were heaped on Christian when he became a pilgrim, that he will regard them all as done to himself, Luke 10:16; and no wonder, for it was out of the love he had for his Prince that he ventured as he did.

I dare say, I said; I am glad of it. I am glad for the poor man’s sake, because he now has rest from his labor, and because he now reaps the benefit of his tears with joy, and because he has got beyond the gunshot of his enemies and is out of the reach of those who hate him. Rev 14:13; Ps 126:5, 6. I am also glad that a rumor of these things is spreading through this country; who knows whether it may have some good effect on some who are left behind? But please, sir, while it is fresh in my mind, do you hear anything of his wife and children? Poor hearts! I wonder what they are doing.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: Who? Christiana and her sons? They are likely to do as well as Christian did himself; for though they all played the fool at first, and would not be persuaded at all by either the tears or the pleading of Christian, second thoughts have worked wonderfully with them. So they have packed up and gone after him too.]

Better and better, I said; but what! Wife and children and all?

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: It is true. I can give you an account of the matter, for I was on the spot at the time, and knew the whole affair thoroughly.]

Then, I said, it seems a man may report it as the truth.

#speech[#smallcaps[Mr. Sagacity]: You need not be afraid to say so. I mean that they have all gone on pilgrimage, both the good woman and her four boys. And since we are, as I see, going a good way together, I will give you an account of the whole matter.]

As for this Christiana (for that was her name from the day she and her children took up a pilgrim’s life), after her husband had gone over the river and she could hear no more of him, her thoughts began to work in her mind. First, she had lost her husband, and the loving bond between them was utterly broken. For you know, he said to me, nature cannot help but fill the living with many a heavy thought when they remember the loss of those they love. So this loss of her husband cost her many a tear. But that was not all; for Christiana also began to ask herself whether her unbecoming behavior towards her husband was not one reason why she saw him no more, and why he was taken from her in such a way. And at this there came into her mind, in swarms, all her unkind, unnatural, and ungodly conduct towards her dear husband, which also clogged her conscience and loaded her with guilt. She was, moreover, much broken by recalling her husband’s restless groans, salt tears, and laments over himself, and how she had hardened her heart against all his pleading and his loving persuasion of her and her sons to go with him. Indeed, there was nothing that Christian either said to her or did in front of her, all the while his burden hung on his back, but it came back to her like a flash of lightning and tore apart the covering of her heart; especially that bitter outcry of his, “What shall I do to be saved?” rang in her ears most mournfully.

Then she said to her children, Sons, we are all undone. I have sinned away your father, and he is gone. He would have had us with him, but I would not go myself; I have also kept you from life. At that the boys burst into tears and cried out to go after their father. Oh, said Christiana, if only it had been our lot to go with him! Then it would have gone well with us, far better than it is likely to now. For though I once foolishly imagined, about your father’s troubles, that they came from some foolish fancy of his, or because he was overrun with melancholy, yet now I cannot get it out of my mind that they sprang from another cause, namely, that the Light of Light was given him, Jas 1:23–25; and by its help, as I now see, he has escaped the snares of death. Prov 14:27. Then they all wept again, and cried out, Oh, woe to the day!

The next night Christiana had a dream; and behold, she saw what seemed to be a broad parchment opened before her, in which the sum of her ways was recorded; and the crimes, as she thought, looked very black against her. Then she cried out aloud in her sleep, “Lord, have mercy on me, a sinner!” Luke 18:13; and the little children heard her.

After this she thought she saw two very ill-favored ones standing by her bedside and saying, What shall we do with this woman? For she cries out for mercy, waking and sleeping. If she is allowed to go on as she has begun, we will lose her as we have lost her husband. So we must, one way or another, try to draw her mind away from thoughts of what will be hereafter, or else all the world cannot keep her from becoming a pilgrim.

Now she woke in a great sweat, and trembling as well; but after a while she fell asleep again. And then she thought she saw Christian, her husband, in a place of bliss among many immortals, with a harp in his hand, standing and playing it before One who sat on a throne with a rainbow around his head. She also saw him, as it seemed, bow his head with his face to the pavement that was under his Prince’s feet, saying, “I heartily thank my Lord and King for bringing me into this place.” Then a company of those who stood round about shouted and harped with their harps; but no man living could tell what they said except Christian and his companions.

Next morning, when she was up, had prayed to God, and had talked with her children a while, someone knocked hard at the door; and she called out to him, “If you come in God’s name, come in.” So he said, “Amen,” and opened the door, and greeted her with, “Peace be to this house.” When he had done that, he said, “Christiana, do you know why I have come?” Then she blushed and trembled; and her heart began to grow warm with longing to know where he came from, and what his errand to her was. So he said to her, “My name is Secret; I dwell with those that are on high. It is said where I dwell that you seem to have a desire to go there; there is also a report that you are aware of the evil you have done to your husband in the past, in hardening your heart against his way, and in keeping these babes in their ignorance. Christiana, the Merciful One has sent me to tell you that he is a God ready to forgive, and that he delights to multiply the pardon of offenses. He also wants you to know that he invites you to come into his presence, to his table, and that he will feed you with the fat of his house, and with the heritage of Jacob your father.

“There is Christian, who was your husband, with legions more, his companions, always looking on that face that gives life to those who look on it; and they will all be glad when they hear the sound of your feet stepping over your Father’s threshold.”

At this Christiana was greatly abashed, and bowed her head to the ground. The visitor went on, and said, “Christiana, here is also a letter for you, which I have brought from your husband’s King.” So she took it and opened it, and it smelled like the best perfume. Song 1:3. It was also written in letters of gold. The contents of the letter were these: that the King wanted her to do as Christian her husband had done; for that was the way to come to his city, and to live in his presence with joy for ever. At this the good woman was quite overcome; so she cried out to her visitor, Sir, will you take me and my children with you, so that we too may go and worship the King?

Then the visitor said, Christiana, the bitter comes before the sweet. You must enter this Celestial City through troubles, as he who went before you did. So I advise you to do as Christian your husband did: go to the Wicket-gate over there, across the plain, for it stands at the head of the way you must go up; and I wish you all good speed. I also advise you to put this letter in your bosom, and to read it to yourself and to your children until you#super[+] have got it by heart; for it is one of the songs that you must sing while you are in this house of your pilgrimage, Ps 119:54; and you must also hand it in at the far gate.

Now I saw in my dream that this old gentleman, as he told me the story, seemed himself to be greatly moved by it. He went on, and said, So Christiana called her sons together, and began to speak to them like this: “My sons, as you may see, I have lately been in much trouble in my soul about the death of your father; not that I doubt his happiness at all, for I am now satisfied that he is well. I have also been much moved by thoughts of my own state and yours, which I truly believe is miserable by nature. My conduct towards your father in his distress is also a great load on my conscience; for I hardened both my own heart and yours against him, and refused to go with him on pilgrimage.

“The thought of these things would kill me outright now, if it were not for a dream I had last night, and for the encouragement this stranger has given me this morning. Come, my children, let us pack up and be off to the gate that leads to the Celestial country, so that we may see your father, and be with him and his companions in peace, according to the laws of that land.”

Then her children burst into tears, for joy that their mother’s heart was so inclined. So their visitor bid them farewell, and they began to get ready to set out on their journey.

But while they were getting ready to go, two of the women who were Christiana’s neighbors came up to her house and knocked at her door. She said to them, as before, If you come in God’s name, come in. At this the women were stunned; for they were not used to hearing this kind of language, or to hearing it fall from Christiana’s lips. Still, they came in; and there they found the good woman getting ready to leave her house.

So they began by saying, Neighbor, please, what do you mean by this?

Christiana answered, saying to the eldest of them, whose name was Mrs. Timorous, I am getting ready for a journey.

This Timorous was the daughter of the man who met Christian on the Hill of Difficulty, and wanted him to go back for fear of the lions.

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: What journey, may I ask?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Why, to go after my good husband. And with that she began to weep.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: I hope not, good neighbor. Please, for your poor children’s sake, do not throw yourself away in so unwomanly a fashion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: No, my children will go with me; not one of them is willing to stay behind.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: I wonder with all my heart what or who has put this into your head!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: O neighbor, if you only knew as much as I do, I have no doubt that you would go along with me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: Tell me, what new knowledge have you got, that draws your mind away from your friends like this, and tempts you to go nobody knows where?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christiana]: Then Christiana replied, I have been deeply afflicted since my husband left me, but especially since he went over the river. But what troubles me most is my churlish conduct towards him when he was in his distress. Besides, I am now as he was then; nothing will do for me but going on pilgrimage. I was dreaming last night that I saw him. Oh, that my soul were with him! He lives in the presence of the King of the country; he sits and eats with him at his table; he has become a companion of immortals, and has now been given a house to live in, compared with which the best palace on earth seems to me no better than a dunghill. 2 Cor 5:1–4. The Prince of the place has also sent for me, with the promise of a welcome if I come to him; his messenger was here just now, and has brought me a letter, which invites me to come. And with that she pulled out her letter, and read it, and said to them, Now what will you say to this?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: Oh, the madness that has possessed you and your husband, to rush into such difficulties! You have heard, I am sure, what your husband met with almost at the very first step he took on his way, as our neighbor Obstinate can still testify, for he went along with him; yes, and Pliable too, until they, like wise men, were afraid to go any further. We also heard, besides, how he met with the lions, Apollyon, the Shadow of Death, and many other things. Nor should you forget the danger he met with at Vanity Fair. For if he, though a man, was so hard pressed, what can you do, being only a poor woman? Consider also that these four sweet babes are your children, your flesh and your bones. So, though you should be so rash as to throw yourself away, yet for the sake of the fruit of your body, stay at home.]

But Christiana said to her, Do not tempt me, my neighbor. I now have the means put into my hands to get gain, and I would be a fool of the greatest size if I had no heart to seize the opportunity. And as for all these troubles you tell me I am likely to meet with on the way, they are so far from discouraging me that they show I am in the right. The bitter must come before the sweet, and that will also make the sweet the sweeter. So, since you did not come to my house in God’s name, as I said, please be gone, and do not disturb me any further.

Then Timorous railed at her, and said to her companion, Come, neighbor Mercy, let us leave her to herself, since she scorns our advice and our company. But Mercy hesitated, and could not so readily go along with her neighbor, and that for a twofold reason. 1. Her heart yearned over Christiana. So she said to herself, If my neighbor must go, I will go a little way with her and help her. 2. Her heart yearned over her own soul; for what Christiana had said had taken some hold on her mind. So she said to herself again, I will have more talk with this Christiana yet; and if I find truth and life in what she says, I myself will go with her too, with all my heart. So Mercy began to answer her neighbor Timorous like this:

#speech[#smallcaps[Mercy]: Neighbor, I did indeed come with you to see Christiana this morning; and since she is, as you see, taking her last farewell of the country, I think I will walk a little way with her this sunny morning, to help her on her way. But she did not tell her about her second reason; she kept it to herself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: Well, I see you have a mind to go off fooling too; but take care in time, and be wise: while we are out of danger, we are out; but when we are in, we are in.]

So Mrs. Timorous went back to her house, and Christiana set out on her journey. But when Timorous had got home, she sends for some of her neighbors, namely, Mrs. Bat’s-Eyes, Mrs. Inconsiderate, Mrs. Light-Mind, and Mrs. Know-Nothing. So when they had come to her house, she starts telling them the story of Christiana and her intended journey. And this is how she began her tale:

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: Neighbors, having little to do this morning, I went to pay Christiana a visit; and when I came to the door I knocked, as you know is our custom; and she answered, If you come in God’s name, come in. So in I went, thinking all was well; but when I came in, I found her getting ready to leave the town, she and her children too. So I asked her what she meant by that. And she told me, in short, that she now had a mind to go on pilgrimage, as her husband did. She also told me of a dream she had, and how the King of the country where her husband was had sent her a letter inviting her to come there.]

Then Mrs. Know-Nothing said, And what! Do you think she will go?

#speech[#smallcaps[Timorous]: Oh yes, go she will, whatever comes of it; and I think I know it by this: what was my great argument to persuade her to stay at home (namely, the troubles she was likely to meet with on the way) is one great argument with her for pressing on with her journey. For she told me in so many words, The bitter goes before the sweet; yes, and since it does, it makes the sweet the sweeter.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Mrs. Bat’s-Eyes]: Oh, this blind and foolish woman! she said; and will she not take warning from her husband’s afflictions? For my part, I can see that if he were here again, he would be content to keep his skin whole, and never run so many risks for nothing.]

Mrs. Inconsiderate also replied, saying, Away with such fanciful fools from the town! A good riddance of her, I say, for my part. If she stayed where she lives and kept this mind of hers, who could live quietly next to her? For she will either be gloomy, or unneighborly, or talk of such matters as no sensible person can stand. So, for my part, I will never be sorry she has gone; let her go, and let someone better come in her place. It was never a good world since these whimsical fools lived in it.

Then Mrs. Light-Mind added this: Come, enough of this kind of talk. Yesterday I was at Madam Wanton’s, where we were as merry as the maids. For who do you think was there but me and Mrs. Love-the-Flesh, and three or four more, with Mrs. Lechery, Mrs. Filth, and some others; so there we had music and dancing, and whatever else was fitting to fill up the pleasure. And I dare say my lady herself is an admirably well-bred gentlewoman, and Mr. Lechery is as pretty a fellow.
